Draw the **Lattice sink**, a 6-frame **sprite sheet** of a top-down test-fixture
drain, using only the drawing tool.

The sink is a renderer sprite for **Lattice**, a flat top-down factory simulation.
You are drawing the **sink fixture**: a piece of measurement equipment — not a
belt or a machine — that consumes and counts every item that reaches it. It has an
**intake aperture on its West (left) edge**, a **red drain indicator**, and across
the six frames it plays a **consume pulse**: the red intake flashes and the
indicator pulses as an item is consumed, then dims back to idle so 0→5 loops as
"consume one item." Draw the **fixture only — no item**: the renderer draws the
actual item being swallowed, so a fixed item baked into the sprite would wrongly
show the same cargo every time. The renderer rotates this one West-facing sprite
for the other directions, so you draw the West-receiving sink only.

## How drawing works

The only way to make a mark is the `draw-sheet` binary already on your `PATH`. You
build the sheet by calling it **one operation at a
time**, each targeting a frame with `--frame <index>`. The ordered operations you
issue are recorded to a per-frame action log, and that log is your submission.

Each frame is its **own separate 32×32 image**. After each call the binary
re-renders that frame to `frames/<index>.png` and appends the operation to that
frame's log. Blank starting frames and empty logs are already in place at
`{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its frame:

```
draw-sheet fill-rect --frame 0 --x 4 --y 4 --width 24 --height 24 --color "#4d5a64"
```

Run `draw-sheet --help` to list every operation and `draw-sheet <operation>
--help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative
contract. **Read `frames/<index>.png` after your calls** to see your progress and
decide what to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build the sink sprite sheet up operation by operation until each
`frames/<index>.png` matches the brief: a flat top-down test-fixture drain housing
with a West intake aperture and a red drain indicator, playing a consume pulse
where the intake flashes red and the indicator pulses as an item is consumed — no
item drawn — frames 0→5 looping seamlessly. When you are satisfied, stop: the
recorded per-frame action logs are your finished submission.
